from common import *
mc('R1.1','sl','Reading an energy profile','The diagram gives energies relative to an arbitrary common reference. What is the forward activation energy?', ['35 kJ mol⁻¹','55 kJ mol⁻¹','80 kJ mol⁻¹','115 kJ mol⁻¹'],2,'The forward activation energy is the peak minus the reactant energy: 115−35=80 kJ mol⁻¹.',figure=energy(35,115,-20))
mc('R1.1','sl','A cooling reaction mixture','A reaction carried out in an insulated cup causes the solution temperature to fall. Assuming the reaction dominates the temperature change, which statement is correct?', ['The reaction releases heat to the solution','The reaction absorbs heat from the solution','The reaction must be fast at room temperature','No energy is transferred'],1,'The chemical system absorbs energy from the solution, so the measured solution cools. The reaction is endothermic under the stated assumptions.')
mc('R1.1','sl','Doubling the amount reacted','A reaction releases 12.0 kJ when 0.0300 mol of the specified reactant is consumed. What is its enthalpy change per mole of that reactant?', ['−0.360 kJ mol⁻¹','+400 kJ mol⁻¹','−400 kJ mol⁻¹','−40.0 kJ mol⁻¹'],2,'ΔH=−12.0/0.0300=−400 kJ mol⁻¹. The minus sign indicates heat released.')
mc('R1.2','sl','Breaking and making bonds','Which statement correctly describes bond enthalpy accounting for a gas-phase reaction?', ['Breaking bonds releases energy and making bonds absorbs energy','Both processes release energy','Both processes absorb energy','Breaking bonds absorbs energy and making bonds releases energy'],3,'Energy is needed to separate bonded atoms; forming bonds lowers potential energy. ΔH is estimated by bonds broken minus bonds formed.')
mc('R1.2','sl','Reversing a step',r'For $\mathrm{A\rightarrow2B}$, $\Delta H=+84\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ of reaction. What is $\Delta H$ for $\mathrm{B\rightarrow\tfrac12A}$?', ['+42 kJ mol⁻¹','−42 kJ mol⁻¹','−84 kJ mol⁻¹','+168 kJ mol⁻¹'],1,'Reverse the reaction to reverse the sign, then halve all coefficients and the enthalpy: −84/2=−42 kJ mol⁻¹.')
mc('R1.3','sl','Incomplete combustion','Which carbon-containing product provides evidence that a hydrocarbon underwent incomplete rather than complete combustion?', ['CO₂ only','CO','H₂O','O₂'],1,'CO is a product of incomplete oxidation of carbon. Complete hydrocarbon combustion produces CO₂ and water.')
mc('R1.3','sl','An acidic hydrogen fuel cell','Which half-equation occurs at the anode of a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell with an acidic electrolyte?', ['O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O','H₂ → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻','2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻','H₂ + 2e⁻ → 2H⁻'],1,'Oxidation occurs at the anode: hydrogen loses electrons to form protons in an acidic electrolyte.')
mc('R1.3','sl','Comparing carbon intensity','Fuel A releases 1000 kJ and produces 2.00 mol CO₂ per mole. Fuel B releases 1500 kJ and produces 2.50 mol CO₂ per mole. Which has the lower CO₂ production per unit energy?', ['A, because it forms fewer moles per mole of fuel','B, because 2.50/1500 is less than 2.00/1000','Both are equal','It cannot be decided from these data'],1,'A produces 0.00200 mol kJ⁻¹; B produces 0.00167 mol kJ⁻¹. Comparisons must use equal energy output.')
mc('R1.4','ahl','Temperature and feasibility',r'A reaction has $\Delta H>0$ and $\Delta S>0$, treated as temperature-independent. When is $\Delta G<0$?', ['At sufficiently high temperature','At sufficiently low temperature only','At every temperature','At no temperature'],0,'ΔG=ΔH−TΔS becomes negative when TΔS exceeds ΔH. Temperature must be in kelvin and the units must match.')
mc('R1.4','ahl','Standard and actual Gibbs energy',r'At fixed temperature a reaction has $\Delta G^\circ>0$. Which statement is necessarily true?', ['The forward reaction can never occur','Its equilibrium constant is less than 1','Its forward rate is zero','Every mixture has ΔG>0'],1,'ΔG°=−RT ln K, so positive ΔG° implies K<1. The actual ΔG depends on Q and can be negative for a suitable starting mixture.')
wr('R1.1','sl','Measuring neutralization heat',r'$50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl is mixed with $50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Both start at 21.0°C; the final temperature is 27.4°C. Assume density $1.00\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$, heat capacity $4.18\ \mathrm{J\,g^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and negligible cup heat capacity.',[('Calculate the heat gained by the solution and the molar enthalpy of neutralization per mole of water formed.',[('M1','q=100.0×4.18×6.4=2675.2 J=2.6752 kJ.'),('M1','n(H₂O)=0.0500 mol; the acid and base are present in stoichiometric amounts.'),('A1','ΔH=−2.6752/0.0500=−53.5 kJ mol⁻¹; accept −54 kJ mol⁻¹ to 2 significant figures.')]),('Explain how heat loss affects the value, and suggest one practical improvement.',[('R1','Heat loss reduces the measured temperature rise, so the result is less negative than the true value.'),('A1','Improve insulation/use a lid, or extrapolate the cooling curve back to mixing time.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R1.1','sl','Including calorimeter heat capacity',r'A reaction warms $80.0\ \mathrm{g}$ of solution by $5.20\ \mathrm{K}$. The solution heat capacity is $4.18\ \mathrm{J\,g^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and the calorimeter heat capacity is $35.0\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The reaction consumes $0.0250\ \mathrm{mol}$ of the specified reactant.',[('Calculate the heat absorbed by the solution and calorimeter together.',[('M1','q=(mc+Ccal)ΔT=(80.0×4.18+35.0)×5.20.'),('A1','q=1920.88 J=1.92 kJ.')]),('Calculate the molar reaction enthalpy and explain the bias from ignoring the calorimeter.',[('A1','ΔH=−1.92088/0.0250=−76.8 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','Ignoring heat absorbed by the calorimeter underestimates released heat, producing a value that is less negative.')])])
wr('R1.1','sl','A dissolving salt',r'A $4.00\ \mathrm{g}$ sample of salt of molar mass $80.0\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ dissolves in $96.0\ \mathrm{g}$ of water. The temperature falls by $3.00\ \mathrm{K}$. Treat the final solution heat capacity as $4.18\ \mathrm{J\,g^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate the enthalpy change of solution per mole of salt.',[('M1','Use the final solution mass 100.0 g: q(solution)=100.0×4.18×(−3.00)=−1254 J.'),('M1','q(reaction)=+1.254 kJ; n=4.00/80.0=0.0500 mol.'),('A1','ΔH(solution)=+25.1 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain the expected effect of heat entering from the room during the measurement.',[('R1','Heat entering reduces the observed cooling, so the calculated positive enthalpy is too small.')])])
wr('R1.1','sl','Catalysis on an energy diagram','The solid curve is the uncatalysed pathway and the dashed curve is a catalysed pathway. Energies share an arbitrary common reference.',[('Determine ΔH and the forward activation energies for both pathways.',[('A1','ΔH=−35−20=−55 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('A1','Uncatalysed Ea=120−20=100 kJ mol⁻¹; catalysed Ea=75−20=55 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain why the catalyst changes rate without changing the reaction enthalpy.',[('R1','It provides a pathway with a lower activation barrier.'),('R1','The same reactants and products have the same energy difference; their endpoint energies are unchanged.')])],figure=energy(20,120,-35,75))
wr('R1.2','sl','A Hess-law route',r'Two steps are measured: $\mathrm{A+B\rightarrow C}$, $\Delta H=-68\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; $\mathrm{C+B\rightarrow D}$, $\Delta H=+24\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The letters represent chemical species in internally balanced reaction steps.',[('Determine ΔH for A+2B→D.',[('M1','Add the two steps and cancel intermediate C.'),('A1','ΔH=−68+24=−44 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Determine ΔH for D→A+2B and explain why the route does not affect the result.',[('A1','+44 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','Enthalpy is a state function, so the change depends only on initial and final states.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('R1.2','sl','Estimating a hydrogenation enthalpy',r'For $\mathrm{CH_2=CH_2(g)+H_2(g)\rightarrow CH_3CH_3(g)}$, use mean bond enthalpies in kJ mol⁻¹: C=C 612, H–H 436, C–C 346, C–H 413.',[('Estimate the reaction enthalpy.',[('M1','Net bonds broken: one C=C and one H–H; net bonds formed: one C–C and two C–H.'),('M1','ΔH≈612+436−346−2×413.'),('A1','ΔH≈−124 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain why the estimate may differ from a precise experimental gas-phase value.',[('R1','Mean bond enthalpies average different molecular environments; the actual bond energies in these molecules need not equal the averages.')])])
wr('R1.2','ahl','Formation enthalpies in combustion',r'Use standard formation enthalpies in kJ mol⁻¹: CH₄(g) −74.8, CO₂(g) −393.5 and H₂O(l) −285.8.',[('Write the complete combustion equation and calculate its standard reaction enthalpy.',[('A1','CH₄(g)+2O₂(g)→CO₂(g)+2H₂O(l).'),('M1','ΔH°=[−393.5+2(−285.8)]−[−74.8+0].'),('A1','ΔH°=−890.3 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain why changing the product water to vapour makes the combustion enthalpy less negative.',[('R1','Water vapour has higher enthalpy than liquid water at the same temperature.'),('R1','Energy is needed to vaporize the water, so less heat is released when vapour rather than liquid is produced.')])])
wr('R1.2','ahl','An incomplete Born–Haber calculation',r'For an ionic solid MX, the formation enthalpy is −410 kJ mol⁻¹. The steps to gaseous ions are: metal atomization +105, half the X–X dissociation +120, first ionization +500 and electron affinity −350 kJ mol⁻¹. Lattice formation is defined as gaseous ions forming one mole of solid.',[('Calculate the lattice formation enthalpy.',[('M1','−410=105+120+500−350+L.'),('A1','L=−785 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('State the lattice dissociation enthalpy and explain why the electron-affinity sign must not be changed merely because the process involves an electron.',[('A1','+785 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','The supplied electron affinity describes an exothermic electron-gain step. Its sign is determined by that energy change and the direction used in the cycle.')])])
wr('R1.2','ahl','A doubly charged lattice',r'For MO(s), ΔH°f=−600 kJ mol⁻¹. Supplied steps in kJ mol⁻¹ are metal atomization +150, first and second ionization +740 and +1450, half oxygen bond dissociation +250, and first and second oxygen electron affinities −140 and +780.',[('Determine the lattice formation enthalpy for M²⁺(g)+O²⁻(g)→MO(s).',[('M1','Total of the supplied gas-ion steps=150+740+1450+250−140+780=3230.'),('A1','L=−600−3230=−3830 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain why the second electron affinity of oxygen is positive and why forming the solid can still be favourable.',[('R1','An electron is added to an already negative ion, so electron–electron electrostatic repulsion must be overcome.'),('R1','The large energy release on forming the 2+/2− ionic lattice can offset the endothermic steps.')])])
wr('R1.3','sl','Energy delivered by two fuels','The following idealized comparison excludes production and transport emissions.'+table(['Fuel','Energy / kJ mol⁻¹','Molar mass / g mol⁻¹','CO₂ / mol per mol fuel'],[('Methanol',726,32.0,1),('Propane',2220,44.0,3)]),[('Calculate the energy per gram of each fuel.',[('A1','Methanol: 726/32.0=22.7 kJ g⁻¹.'),('A1','Propane: 2220/44.0=50.5 kJ g⁻¹.')]),('Calculate the mass of CO₂ released per MJ of useful heat if both operate at 80.0% efficiency. Use M(CO₂)=44.0 g mol⁻¹.',[('M1','Use CO₂ mass per mole divided by 0.800 times the energy per mole, then multiply by 1000.'),('A1','Methanol: 75.8 g MJ⁻¹; propane: 74.3 g MJ⁻¹.')]),('State one reason this calculation cannot establish which fuel has the lower full life-cycle impact.',[('R1','Production route, feedstock renewal, transport, leakage and processing energy are excluded; any one relevant factor is accepted.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R1.3','sl','Methanol in an acidic fuel cell',r'Methanol is oxidized to carbon dioxide in an acidic fuel cell. Oxygen is reduced to water.',[('Write balanced anode and cathode half-equations using six electrons in each.',[('A1','Anode: CH₃OH + H₂O → CO₂ + 6H⁺ + 6e⁻.'),('A1','Cathode: 1.5O₂ + 6H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 3H₂O.')]),('Combine them into an overall equation and identify the direction of electron flow through the external circuit.',[('A1','CH₃OH + 1.5O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; accept doubled integer coefficients.'),('A1','From the methanol oxidation electrode (anode) to the oxygen reduction electrode (cathode).')])])
wr('R1.3','sl','A biofuel carbon claim','A producer describes ethanol made from crops as completely carbon neutral.',[('Write a balanced photosynthesis equation showing carbon uptake and a balanced ethanol combustion equation showing carbon release.',[('A1','6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light energy supplied.'),('A1','C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.')]),('Evaluate the claim using two distinct life-cycle considerations.',[('R1','Crop growth can recapture carbon released on burning, but only if biomass is regrown and land carbon stocks are maintained.'),('R1','Fertilizer manufacture, farming, fermentation/distillation and transport can require fossil energy; land-use change can also release stored carbon. Accept two distinct relevant considerations.')])])
wr('R1.4','ahl','A temperature threshold',r'A reaction has $\Delta H^\circ=+48.0\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\Delta S^\circ=+160\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$. Treat both as constant over the interval considered.',[('Calculate ΔG° at 298 K.',[('M1','Convert entropy to 0.160 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and use ΔG°=ΔH°−TΔS°.'),('A1','ΔG°=48.0−298×0.160=+0.320 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Determine the threshold temperature and state which side is standard-state spontaneous.',[('M1','Set ΔG°=0: T=48.0/0.160.'),('A1','T=300 K; ΔG°<0 above 300 K. At 300 K it is zero, not negative.')]),('Explain why this does not predict a rapid reaction just above the threshold.',[('R1','Rate depends on the activation barrier and kinetic pathway; thermodynamic feasibility does not specify the rate.')])])
wr('R1.4','ahl','Entropy of vaporization',r'For a liquid at its normal boiling temperature of $350\ \mathrm{K}$, $\Delta H_{vap}=28.0\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Assume liquid and vapour are at equilibrium.',[('Calculate the molar entropy change of vaporization.',[('M1','At phase equilibrium, ΔG=0, so ΔS=ΔH/T.'),('A1','ΔS=28000/350=80.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain the sign of ΔS in terms of energy distribution and accessible arrangements.',[('R1','The gas phase has many more accessible arrangements / positional microstates than the liquid.'),('R1','Energy and particles can be distributed among more possible states, so entropy increases.')])])
wr('R1.4','ahl','Standard entropy from a table',r'For $\mathrm{N_2(g)+3H_2(g)\rightarrow2NH_3(g)}$, use the supplied rounded standard molar entropies.'+table(['Species','S° / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹'],[('N₂(g)',192),('H₂(g)',131),('NH₃(g)',193)]),[('Calculate ΔS° for the reaction as written.',[('M1','ΔS°=2(193)−[192+3(131)].'),('A1','ΔS°=−199 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.')]),('Given ΔH°=−92.0 kJ mol⁻¹ of reaction, calculate ΔG° at 400 K and comment on the effect of increasing temperature.',[('A1','ΔG°=−92.0−400(−0.199)=−12.4 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','Because ΔS° is negative, increasing T makes ΔG° less negative and eventually positive, under the constant-ΔH°/ΔS° approximation.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R1.4','ahl','Equilibrium from Gibbs energy',r'At $298\ \mathrm{K}$ a reaction has $\Delta G^\circ=-11.4\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$ and a dimensionless equilibrium constant.',[('Calculate K.',[('M1','ln K=−ΔG°/RT=11400/(8.31×298)=4.6034.'),('A1','K=99.8, approximately 1.00×10².')]),('A mixture at this temperature has Q=200. Predict the spontaneous direction and justify your answer.',[('R1','Q>K, so there is excess product relative to equilibrium and the reverse direction is favoured.'),('R1','ΔG(forward)=RT ln(Q/K)>0; this concerns thermodynamic direction rather than rate.')])])
wr('R1.4','ahl','Actual Gibbs energy in a mixture',r'At $310\ \mathrm{K}$ a reaction has $\Delta G^\circ=+5.00\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. A mixture has dimensionless $Q=0.0200$. Use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate ΔG for the forward reaction in this mixture.',[('M1','ΔG=ΔG°+RT ln Q=5000+(8.31)(310)ln(0.0200), in joules.'),('A1','ΔG=−5.08 kJ mol⁻¹ (approximately −5078 J mol⁻¹).')]),('Explain why this result does not contradict the positive standard Gibbs energy.',[('R1','Standard Gibbs energy refers to standard-state activities, for which Q=1.'),('R1','The actual mixture has very little product relative to reactant; the negative RT ln Q term can outweigh ΔG°.')])])
wr('R1.4','ahl','Extracting thermodynamics from a graph','Constructed standard Gibbs-energy data are approximately linear over this range.'+table(['T / K','ΔG° / kJ mol⁻¹'],[(250,-10),(300,-5),(350,0),(400,5)]),[('Determine ΔS° and ΔH° from the data.',[('M1','The gradient of ΔG° against T is −ΔS°: (5−(−10))/(400−250)=0.100 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.'),('A1','ΔS°=−100 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.'),('A1','ΔH°=ΔG°+TΔS°=−10+250(−0.100)=−35.0 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('State the standard-state spontaneous temperature range under the linear model and one limitation of extrapolation.',[('A1','Below 350 K, where ΔG°<0.'),('R1','ΔH° and ΔS° can vary with temperature, and phase changes can invalidate the straight-line extrapolation.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(250,-10),(300,-5),(350,0),(400,5)],'#186c93')],'Temperature / K','ΔG° / kJ mol⁻¹',ylim=(-15,10)))
wr('R1.1','sl','An enthalpy measurement uncertainty',r'An experiment gives a temperature rise of $4.8\ \mathrm{K}$. Each of the initial and final temperature readings has maximum uncertainty $\pm0.1\ \mathrm{K}$. The solution mass has 0.5% uncertainty and the reacting amount has 1.0% uncertainty. Treat heat capacity as exact and add percentage uncertainties.',[('Calculate the maximum percentage uncertainty in the temperature rise and in the molar enthalpy.',[('M1','The temperature difference has uncertainty ±0.2 K.'),('A1','Temperature-rise percentage uncertainty=0.2/4.8×100=4.17%.'),('A1','Molar enthalpy percentage uncertainty=4.17+0.5+1.0=5.67%.')]),('For a result of −42.0 kJ mol⁻¹, give the absolute uncertainty and identify the most effective measurement to improve.',[('A1','Absolute uncertainty=42.0×0.0567≈2.4 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','Improve the temperature-difference measurement, which has the largest relative contribution; accept a larger measurable temperature rise if other conditions remain valid.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R1.2','ahl','Finding an unknown formation enthalpy',r'Ethanol burns according to $\mathrm{C_2H_5OH(l)+3O_2(g)\rightarrow2CO_2(g)+3H_2O(l)}$, with $\Delta H_c^\circ=-1367\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Use $\Delta H_f^\circ[\mathrm{CO_2(g)}]=-394$ and $\Delta H_f^\circ[\mathrm{H_2O(l)}]=-286\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Determine the standard formation enthalpy of ethanol.',[('M1','−1367=2(−394)+3(−286)−ΔH°f(ethanol).'),('A1','ΔH°f(ethanol)=−279 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Write the formation equation with correct standard-state reactants and explain the coefficient of ethanol.',[('A1','2C(graphite,s)+3H₂(g)+½O₂(g)→C₂H₅OH(l).'),('R1','A standard formation enthalpy refers to forming one mole of compound from its elements in their standard states.')])])
save('R1')
